WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.801
-Rachel Dratch!
Oh, my gosh, pal.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.534
-Let me fix myself here.
Hi! Pally!

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:05.834
-Thank you
for playing "Password."

00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:07.734
-Oh, my God, that was, like,
the highlight of my year.

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:10.334
-Could you believe us?
-I love those kind of games.

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:11.701
-But could you believe --
-We really had

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:13.534
a psychic connection going on.
-That was unbelievable.

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:15.367
We almost didn't even
need to look at the cards.

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:16.901
-I know.
We were like -- Yeah.

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.701
I feel like that's years of
"SNL" together.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:20.133
-I think it's what it is.
-I don't know.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.033
-I can't even tell you how
psyched I am and happy for you.

00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:24.968
You're nominated for
a Tony award, buddy.

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:26.701
I mean, come on!
-I know.

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:28.100
-Who does it better than you?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.701
Who does it better
than Rachel Dratch?

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:31.501
Come on. We --
-Thank you.

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:33.234
-We've known each other
for over 20 years.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:34.567
-Oh, my God, so many years.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.133
-Would you ever see us,
back then, where we are now?

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:38.934
-Well, you know what?
it's funny

00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:40.267
'cause I was thinking
about this,

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.367
because you were always so good
at chatting up the host.

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.901
And we'd be waiting around
during rehearsals,

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:48.934
and you're standing there next
to some really famous person.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:51.667
And you were always really good
at making them feel

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.167
comfortable and talking,
so I think it's perfect

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.000
that you're doing this now,
'cause it's your wheelhouse.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.067
But I was always, like, so shy.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.300
And I'd stand next to them
and just, like...

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.100
Like, we'd just
stand there in silence

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.934
and then I'd be like, "Do you
live in New York or L.A.?"

00:01:04.934 --> 00:01:06.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.133
And it was --
-That's your question?

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.033
-I made things so awkward.
-That's your question?

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:11.467
-Yeah, like,
that was my go-to.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.868
Then they'd say an answer,
and I'd be, "Hmm, yeah."

00:01:13.868 --> 00:01:15.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:17.267
I think this is
the perfect job for you.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.400
-But are you more comfortable
now talking to celebrities?

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.133
-Um, maybe a little bit.

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:23.234
But I still -- Yeah, I still,
like, freak out when I...

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:26.400
Actually, like, we got to meet
so many of our comedy heroes.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.767
So I'm more comfortable
with that.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:32.000
But if I see, like, a rock star,
I get like -- really like,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.267
"Oh, my God!"
So I --

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:35.133
I got to meet Elvis Costello
once at this event.

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:37.000
And I was like -- I totally
fanned out totally.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.200
[ Muttering ]
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.567
And then -- Yeah, those are
the people that I really --

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.501
Oh, and I got to meet
Debbie Harry

00:01:43.501 --> 00:01:44.734
'cause we were doing
this thing together.

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:45.868
I was so excited.
-Blondie.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:47.133
-Blondie.
-Cool.

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:48.534
-And I went up to her --
Well, I didn't say anything

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:49.934
the whole time,
because I was just...

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.234
And then, at the very end,
I was like,

00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:53.267
"Um, I just want to say
I'm a really big fan.

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:56.968
And "Parallel Lines" was
the first album I ever bought."

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:59.267
And she was like, "You bitch."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:02.901
Because -- But in fun, because,
like, I was -- I was aging her.

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.334
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, I see, yeah.

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:05.901
-"I was only 7."
Whatever.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.801
-That's what you want to hear
from Debbie Harry.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:10.934
It tracked with
her cool personality.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.467
-You're nominated for a Tony.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:13.934
I love the Tony Awards,
by the way.

00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:16.400
That's one of my
favorite award shows

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.667
out of all the award shows,

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.734
Because I feel like,
for Tony Awards,

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:21.901
they're like, "Hey, you're
nominated for this musical.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:23.901
Do it for us."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:27.100
You know? What?
No other award show does that.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.133
-Right, right, right.
-"Go do it for us.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.501
Let's see if it's good.
It's okay."

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:31.767
You know, it's like, "Wow."
-Yeah.

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:34.267
-But it is all performers,
all entertainers.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.734
Do you think that "SNL"
prepared you for Broadway?

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:39.133
-Well, you know, the thing --
as you know,

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:40.534
the pressure of "SNL" is

00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:42.100
you had to write something
every week.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.501
Or try to write something.
So that was the thing

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:45.834
that was really hard
about "SNL."

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:47.234
And this is just, like,
you show up.

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:49.367
It's all written for you.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.801
-Wow.
-You just have the fun part.

00:02:50.801 --> 00:02:54.300
So it's a lot of fun.
This part that I have

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.801
in this play is like,
I'm kind of the clown of the --

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:58.634
Like, I get to do
a lot of physical stuff.

00:02:58.634 --> 00:03:00.133
And it's really fun.

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.501
It's kind of "SNL" style.
My part is, anyway.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:05.267
-Did your son --
Did Eli freak out

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:06.901
when he heard the news that
you're nominated for a Tony?

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:08.234
-So, I have an 11-year-old son,

00:03:08.234 --> 00:03:09.868
and he did not know
what the Tonys were.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.534
But then I told him,
and then he was excited.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:13.067
-Okay, good.
-And then he went to school.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.200
Then, he went to school,
and he was like,

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.567
"My mom's nominated for a Tony."

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.501
And they also did not know
what a Tony was.

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.234
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:21.100
But also then, I did --
I was on the fence

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.434
about having him come
to the show,

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:25.167
'cause there's a lot of
adult language in this show.

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:27.901
And so I was, kind of like,
"Should I do it or not?"

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:30.267
And then all the "SNL" ladies,
I consulted them.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:31.701
They said, "You got to
have him come to the show."

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:35.000
So he went to the opening night.
And, yeah, it was really fun.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.901
He did learn a lot of new words.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:39.367
But then afterwards, it was cute
because afterwards, he didn't --

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:40.667
he's like, "I don't know why

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:42.701
you made such a big deal
about the words."

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:44.267
And then he was like,
"That was so funny!"

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.300
Like, I was glad I had him
come to the show, but...

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.601
-He laughed.
-Should I tell

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.634
that the next day, my mom
came to the show, too.

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:51.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:54.400
The next day, was walking with
him and my mom down the street.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.901
And he goes, "Well, now I know
another 'C' word,

00:03:57.901 --> 00:03:59.801
besides [Bleep]."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.801 --> 00:04:03.234
-Okay.
-So you can bleep that out.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:06.834
I don't know how he learned
the other word,

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:08.200
but he learned another "C" word.
-Doesn't matter.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.701
-New York City is a
tough place to grow up.

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:11.968
-He's a New York kid.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:15.200
-This is your Broadway debut.
-Maybe we'll cut that out.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.100
-No, yeah.
-So Child Protective Services

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.033
doesn't come calling.
-We can cut that out.

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:22.033
-Or maybe not. Okay.
What?

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:23.868
-This is your --
This is your Broadway debut.

00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:26.367
-It is my Broadway debut, yes.
-And you get nominated.

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:28.200
How did you get involved
with this play?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.200
-Okay, so, Susan Stroman.
-Legend.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.000
-Five-time Tony winner? Yeah.
-Legend.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.734
-She directed us,

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:36.334
and I had done a few readings
with her before.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:39.868
So she called me up to do this.
-She knew you.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:42.968
-This part was open, and
this part looked fun to me.

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:45.300
-I know you've done theater.
-Yeah, I've done

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.734
some off-Broadway stuff,
but this was the first Broadway.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:50.367
-Did you dream of Broadway?
-Yeah, so, when I was little,

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:53.100
like, you know, I saw "Annie"
when I was whatever.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.434
-Yeah.
-10 or something.

00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:56.267
And then I was like,
"How did they get up there?"

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:58.067
This might've been like --
I don't know, did you have --

00:04:58.067 --> 00:05:00.868
maybe not "Annie,"
but maybe you saw, like...

00:05:00.868 --> 00:05:02.868
[ Laughter ]
-No, it was "Annie."

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.834
It was "Annie."
I'm like, "I should be Annie."

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.167
[ Laughter ]
"That's me."

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:07.334
♪ The sun will come out ♪

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:10.367
Danny.
Why can't there be "Danny"?

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:12.767
You know?
-But I don't sing like that.

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.968
So I was never like,
"I'm gonna be like that."

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:17.167
But...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:19.567
-♪ I'm gonna be a star ♪

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.167
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:23.767
I just was like,
"How do they do that?

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.234
How does someone get in this?"
You know.

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:26.934
It was always kind of a dream.
Yeah.

00:05:26.934 --> 00:05:28.167
-Can I read you some reviews?

00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:29.567
And if you don't want
to hear them,

00:05:29.567 --> 00:05:31.000
maybe plug your ears, 'cause
I'm gonna read them anyway.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.968
-Then, yes, you can read them.
-"Rachel Dratch is

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:36.601
one of theater's certifiable
comic treasures.

00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:39.601
Her comic timing is great.
Her line readings are giddy fun.

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:42.133
Her performances are
"roll over and die laughing,"

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.634
irresistibly funny."
From "Variety," about you.

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:46.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:50.000
Awesome.
But I'm still happy.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.367
"This is the role Rachel Dratch
was born for" --

00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:54.300
"The Washington Post."
Yeah, that's not bad.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.434
And then this is my --
"New York Post."

00:05:57.434 --> 00:05:59.400
New York --Come on, that's --
you got to go to New York.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.300
The "New York Post" says,
"The genius Rachel Dratch

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.367
is a riot.
Dratch doesn't have to work

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:06.934
for the laughs.
She gets them by existing."

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:08.801
Bang!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.801
What in the world!

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:12.801
-Thank you for reading those.
Geez.

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:15.834
-Come on, buddy.
Can you tell everyone

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.234
what the play is about?
-I've heard it described

00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:19.634
kind of as a cross between
"Noises Off" and "Veep."

00:06:19.634 --> 00:06:22.400
It's a farce.
There's seven women in it.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.067
It's, like, rollicking crazy.
We're trying --

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:27.601
So, you never see the president
in this.

00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:29.167
He's not really a character,
but he --

00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:30.434
-It's the woman
behind the president.

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:31.734
-The woman behind him,
so we're all trying

00:06:31.734 --> 00:06:33.934
to, like, keep the White House
going or, you know,

00:06:33.934 --> 00:06:35.601
put out all the fires
and all that stuff.

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:37.868
And then madness ensues.
-Yeah.

00:06:37.868 --> 00:06:40.434
-And -- yeah.
-But the first half of the play,

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:42.367
you're pretty --
-The first half of the play,

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:44.734
I act kind of probably
how I did at "SNL."

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:46.801
Like, I mean, in my more --
-Shy?

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:49.067
-In my more shy -- Yeah.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:50.334
So, like, in the first half
of the play,

00:06:50.334 --> 00:06:52.534
I'm this, kind of, shy,
mousy secretary

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:53.934
who just, like, wants
to do right

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:55.200
and always saying she's sorry.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.267
But then I accidentally
take this unnamed drug.

00:07:00.267 --> 00:07:03.033
And then, the second half,
I am like, you know --

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:04.467
-Off the rails.
-Yeah, I'm off the rails,

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:06.634
claiming my power.
It's really fun.

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:08.934
Kind of, like, my id
gets to come out,

00:07:08.934 --> 00:07:11.868
and I take a real journey.
-Ah, I love it, bud.

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:13.133
-Yeah, it's really fun.

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:14.701
-Well, of course,
we have to toast.

00:07:14.701 --> 00:07:16.968
-Ooh.
-Because not often you're --

00:07:16.968 --> 00:07:18.367
-Oh, my gosh.
-One of your best friends

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:20.000
are nominated for a Tony.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.434
I love you so much.
-Thank you. I love you, too.

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:23.801
-You totally deserve this.
Rachel Dratch, everybody!

00:07:23.801 --> 00:07:25.400
Here's to your Tony nomination!

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:26.667
You know I'm rooting for you,
buddy.

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:28.834
Rachel Dratch, "POTUS,"
is playing

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:31.467
now through August 14th
at the Shubert Theatre.

